today curator Katie Barrett shares how  one of the most famous British artists  of all time  took her sketchbook into surgery in  another story from the stories why would  a famous artist be sat in an operating  theatre sketching Barbara Hepworth  daughter was very ill in the 1940s she  had a bone infection disease and was  sent to a surgeon called Norman kapner a  Norman Kepner invited Barbara hope was  to go and draw in surgery because he  felt she must have an affinity with  looking at human body and this sketch  book comes from a series of operations  that she saw at the London clinics that  were to do in an operation called  fenestration of the ear and absolutely  we can this is amazing because it's the  only surviving sketchbook from all of  the observations that did some of them  are very simple you can just see the  head of the surgeon here he's looking  through the equipment then you get on to  other kinds of annotation so she's  marking the equipment that's being used  later on she marks colors so she's doing  these incredibly quickly obviously she's  not allowed to change what's happening  in the surgery she can't make them stop  when they're in a nice calm position so  she's recording what she thinks she  might want to draw later what makes it  stand out as one of her pieces okay so  it's interesting actually this this  period of work is very unusual in  Barbara Hepworth work as a whole because  she didn't tend to do bigger studies she  tended to be very abstract but she wrote  quite a lot about these observations  that she made in the surgery this is  actually the sketch which became the  work in the tate and we can see how  she's got this real interest in hands  you can and faces you can see the hands  and faces and the tools and she was  particularly interested in what she  called the kind of harmony and beauty of  how surgery worked together and she saw  it as naturally making the kind of  composition that she was trying to make  in her sculpture and also the process of  doing an operation where this the  fenestration actually involves hammering  a bit of bone out of the skull uses a  hammer and chisel which are the kind of  tools she of course was using in  sculpture so she was particularly  interested in those kind of parities  between surgery and art
 

